  - family-names: "Luzano"
    given-names: "Warren I."
    affiliation: "University of Science and Technology of Southern Philippines"
abstract: >-
  Python/Numba implementation of a variance-scaling analysis of two classical error terms in
  analytic number theory: the Dirichlet divisor error term Delta(x) = D(x) - x(log x + 2*gamma - 1)
  and the Gauss circle error term P(x) = R(x) - pi*x. For each problem the error term is computed
  exactly on a uniform log-grid of approximately 6.5 million points covering thirteen decades
  x in [10^3, 10^16], and a weighted least-squares regression of log(Var[error]) on the
  appropriate log-axis is fitted with HC3 heteroscedasticity-robust standard errors. The slope
  is 2H, where H is the second-moment (variance-scaling) Hurst exponent. The Dirichlet pipeline
  yields H = 0.251060 +/- 0.000120 with 95% robust CI [0.250825, 0.251295], descending
  monotonically toward 1/4 from above as the upper limit of the fitted range grows from 10^8
  to 10^16. A synthetic validation harness drives the same estimator over ground-truth signals
  for H_true in {0.20, 0.23, 0.25, 0.28, 0.30} with 50 disjoint seeds per value and
  inverse-variance pooling.
